INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (48, 8, 'S3 close-out and platform test', 140, 'The last step of milestone 8 (S3). Part 8 of masterplan 8. Once every other S3 part is implemented, this plan hands the gamemaster one test checklist (Windows, Linux, Deck; controller only; offline; a full game; the save survives a restart), records what they report through a pm decision, and brings the docs in line. Workers own no Windows machine or Deck: they prepare the checklist and record results; they never tick a row themselves. Docs and pm only; no code (failures become tasks in the owning plans).

## Depends on (every task waits)
- Plans 44 "Action layer and focus navigation", 45 "Gamepad input and button glyphs", 46 "Deck layout and controller-only text entry", 47 "Credits screen and generated CREDITS.md", and the plans written by tasks 209 (desktop shell, SW-2), 210 (file saves, SW-3), 211 (local builds, SW-5/SW-6): all their tasks done or dropped.
- Guard at the start of each task: `pm show` of this plan and `pm plans list`. If another milestone-8 plan still has open tasks: if one of them waits for a pending decision, `pm task-pending-decision <this task> <that decision>`; otherwise stop without changes and say so (the queue brings the task back). Never drop tasks of other plans.
- Not the milestone''s "post-milestone cleanup" (pm queues it once nothing is open); this plan doesn''t set the milestone status.

## The checklist
- Lives in `plans/18-steam-integration.md`, new section "S3 platform test" (after "Steam Deck Verified"); one table per platform, columns: check, result (pass / fail / skipped), note. Short intro: build commit, date, artefact names.
- Reuses, doesn''t copy: part 3''s offline start checklist (plan from task 211) is linked or merged in; the "what the gamemaster should try" items in the task notes of plans 45 and 46 (`pm show` of their tasks; plan 46 task 3: real Deck in Game Mode) become rows.
- Before the test (worker checks what it can): artefacts built from one commit (part 3''s command); `qgen validate` clean; `trivia-media sync --status approved` run before the build; `CREDITS.md` in the artefact.
- Platforms: Windows x64 (clean machine, no Node/Python), Linux x64 without Nix, Steam Deck in Game Mode (Linux build added as a non-Steam game; built-in controls only). Optional row: Windows build on the Deck via Proton, marked "if cheap".
- Rows per platform:
  - Offline: network off before install and start; no error, no launcher, no console window.
  - Start: fullscreen; F11/menu toggles windowed and back.
  - Controller only (keyboard and mouse unplugged; Xbox-style pad on Windows/Linux): create a new player with the letter picker; pick an existing player; play to level 12 or a loss.
  - Every joker once, including Paso dialog, Francotirador aim and Cambiazo both levels; the ☰ overlay via Start (volume, presets); every dialog closes with B.
  - Glyphs match the pad (Xbox; PlayStation pad if at hand); after a key press they switch to keyboard prompts and back on a pad press.
  - Media: an image, a video and an audio question play; music and sounds audible.
  - Credits screen opens and scrolls with the pad.
  - Save: quit mid-game and restart → players and burned questions kept; force-quit (Deck: Steam button → exit game) and restart → same.
  - Deck only: text readable at arm''s length (800p), nothing cut off, suspend and resume mid-question.
- Current-game check (once, not per platform, until the Steam build replaces it): `trivia` + browser on the TV with keyboard plays a round as before; `state/` save intact.

## Tasks
1. **Checklist and the test decision**
   - Guard (above). Write the "S3 platform test" section in `18-steam-integration.md` as above, results empty.
   - Run what a worker can (build command, `qgen validate`, artefact contains `CREDITS.md`); note it in the section''s intro.
   - `did=$(pm decision add "S3 test passed?" --body - …)` with the whole checklist in the body (platform tables as Markdown, how to report: copy the tables, fill result and note; "skipped" needs a reason) and options `passed`, `passed with minor issues (list them; they go to the backlog)`, `failed (list the failing rows)`.
   - `pm task-pending-decision` for tasks 2 and 3 of this plan on `did`.
   - Done when: the section exists, the decision is pending, tasks 2 and 3 wait for it.
2. **Record the result**
   - Copy the reported results into the section''s tables (date, build commit); add a D-entry in `plans/decisions.md` only if the answer settles something (e.g. Proton row dropped for good).
   - `passed`: done. `passed with minor issues`: each issue into `plans/backlog.md` with the owning plan file''s task ID prefix; done.
   - `failed`: per failing row a `pm task add` in the plan that owns it (shell/saves/builds plans from tasks 209–211, or 44–47) with the row, the note and how to reproduce, at that plan''s priority; then a new decision "S3 re-test passed?" with only the failed rows (plus the full-game row), `pm task-pending-decision` for this task and task 3 on it; stage and stop without closing. Repeat until passed.
   - Done when: the section shows a passing result for every non-optional row.
3. **Docs close-out** (after task 2)
   - `plans/11-steam.md`: S3 row marked done with the date and the checklist link; ST-4 (release checklist) notes that it starts from the S3 checklist.
   - `plans/14-input.md`, `17-publishing.md`, `18-steam-integration.md`: IN-2..IN-5, PUB-5, SW-2..SW-6 ticked with notes (check, don''t redo, the parts'' own ticks); status lines brought up to date (e.g. `stub` → `draft`, or `agreed` where everything for S3 is decided); `plans/README.md` status column to match.
   - `plans/project.md`: "Steam track" paragraph says S3 done (date); any ticked task ticked there too; open-questions rows that S3 resolved removed.
   - Masterplans 9–11 (S4–S6): if still "tbd.", nothing (their writers read the plan files). If written and S3 changed their scope (SW-6 CI moved to S5, media bundle size or depot layout, Proton or Deck findings for SW-12, letter picker vs. on-screen keyboard for SW-8), add a short dated note with `pm plan edit <id> --body -`. Milestone bodies change only if they name something S3 ruled out; listed in the task note.
   - Done when: the milestone''s "Done when" holds and is cited in the task note (decision id, date); `qgen validate` unaffected.', '2026-10-12T01:03:55Z', 216);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (49, 2, 'Save document, stores and migration (PORT-4, PORT-5)', 590, 'The family''s save moves from `state/game.sqlite` to one JSON document, `state/game.json`. The TS engine reads and writes it through a `Store`, and the Python server becomes a dumb file store behind `GET/PUT /api/save`. Flags move into the same document (pm decision 4, D-59). A one-time migration carries the SQLite tables and `flags.json` over, and the authoring side reads the new file. Source: `plans/12-client-engine.md`, PORT-4, PORT-5, "Migration".

## Depends on
- Plan 18 (PORT-1, done): fixtures were recorded on the untouched SQLite state; nothing here changes `game.py`/`selection.py` behaviour.
- Plan 20 (PORT-3), task 76: `engine/state.ts` (`SaveData`, `PlayerRow`, `GameRow`, `BurnRow`, `GameState`, `SAVE_VERSION = 1`, `emptySave()`, `withDefaults`). Only the TS tasks (3, 5) need it; tasks 1, 2 and 4 are Python and can run now.
- Part 5 (task 59, PORT-7) switches the screens. **It also wires `save.migrate()` into `main.py` startup, in the same change**, see "Switch-over" below.

## Decisions taken here
- **Document:** `SaveData {version: 1, players, games, burned, flags}`: plan 20''s tables as JSON plus `flags: {"<question id>": {flagged_on, player}}` (today''s `flags.json` content, D-47). Still version 1, because no `game.json` exists anywhere yet. Fixture replay compares only `players/games/burned`, so the extra key doesn''t touch parity.
- **Server is a dumb store.** `GET /api/save` returns the file as is, or `404 {"error": "not_found"}` when there is none. `PUT /api/save` replaces it whole. The server checks only the envelope: a JSON object, `version` == 1, `players`/`games`/`burned` lists, `flags` an object, body ≤ 10 MB. Anything else gets `400 {"error": "bad_save", "message"}`. No game logic, no merge.
- **Atomic write:** write `game.json.tmp`, `fsync`, then `os.replace`. On `PermissionError` (Windows: antivirus/backup holding the file), retry 10× with 100 ms sleeps, then `503 {"error": "save_locked"}`. Writes go under the existing `_lock`. JSON written with `ensure_ascii=False`, compact (no indent: games carry full states).
- **One writer.** One TV, one browser tab. No revision check; `ServerStore` serializes its own PUTs (one in flight, later saves coalesce to the newest), so an older document never overwrites a newer one.
- **Where things live:** `Store` and `MemoryStore` go in `engine/store.ts` (pure, no I/O). `ServerStore` goes in `app/client/src/lib/store.ts` (uses `fetch`, so not engine). `FileStore` is plan 18 of the Steam work (desktop shell), out of scope.
  - `interface Store { load(): Promise<SaveData>; save(data: SaveData): Promise<void> }`; `load` returns `emptySave()` when nothing is stored.
  - `engine/save.ts`: `parseSave(json): SaveData` (shape check, `version` > 1 → error "save from a newer game", missing `flags` → `{}`, an upgrade chain hook for later versions); `setFlag(save, qid, flagged, player, now)` → new save (same semantics as `flags.set_flag`: keeps the first `flagged_on`); `flaggedIds(save)` sorted.
- **Migration (`app/server/save.py` `migrate()`)**: runs only when `game.json` is missing and `game.sqlite` exists.
  - Copy `game.sqlite` (with `-wal`/`-shm` if present, via `sqlite3` `backup()`) into a temp file. Run `game.init(con)` on the copy, so legacy schemas (no `player_id` on games, pre-D-28 `burned`) get upgraded the way the server always did. Then dump `players`, `games` (`state` parsed from its JSON text), `burned`, each ordered by its key.
  - Merge `state/flags.json` into `flags` when present.
  - Write `game.json` atomically. **Never** write to `game.sqlite`; it stays where it is as the backup. `flags.json` stays too.
  - Prints one line: `migrated game.sqlite → game.json: N players, N games, N burns, N flags`.
  - No `state/` files at all → nothing to migrate; the first PUT creates `game.json`.
- **Authoring reads, never writes `game.json`** (D-59): new `authoring/tools/save_file.py`, which picks its source by whether `game.json` exists (it exists only after the switch-over):
  - `players()` (names, sorted case-insensitively), `burned_ids(player=None)` (casefold name match; global burns `player_id` null; `KeyError` for an unknown name: the same contract as `selection.burned_ids`), `flags()`.
  - Without `game.json` it falls back to `selection.burned_ids`/SQLite and `flags.json` (today''s behaviour). The fallback goes in PORT-8 (task 60).
  - `overview.py` (`players`, `supply_for`) and `qgen report` (`burned_ids`) use it. The supply computation stays `selection.supply` until PORT-8 replaces it with the Node entry point (D-60).
  - `apply_flags` after the switch: reads `flags` from `game.json`, skips entries already applied, and records the applied ones in `state/flags-applied.json` `{"<qid>": "<flagged_on>"}` (gitignored, written by `trivia-authoring` only). An entry counts as applied when both qid and `flagged_on` match, so unflag + reflag in the game produces a new review. Before the switch it keeps today''s `flags.json` path.
- **Decision entry** (next D number): the save is one JSON document written whole by the client; the server only checks the envelope; flags live in it; authoring keeps its own applied-flags ledger; migration on a copy, `.sqlite` kept.

## Switch-over (hand-off to part 5)
- Until part 5, `game.py` still writes `game.sqlite`. Running the migration earlier would leave a stale `game.json`, and the family''s games played in between would be lost at the switch.
- So `migrate()` and the `/api/save` routes land here. `main.py` calls `save.migrate()` before `serve_forever` in part 5''s switch-over change, and not before then. Write that into `plans/12-client-engine.md` "Migration".
- Rollback: delete `game.json` and go back to the commit before the switch; `game.sqlite` is untouched.

## Files
- `app/server/save.py` (new): `FILE`, `load()`, `write(doc)` (validate, atomic, retry), `migrate()`.
- `app/server/main.py`: `GET /api/save`, `PUT /api/save` (`do_PUT`; `http_base.read_body` reused), docstring route list. No startup call yet.
- `app/client/src/engine/save.ts`, `engine/store.ts`, `engine/save.test.ts`, `engine/state.ts` (`flags` in `SaveData`, `emptySave()`).
- `app/client/src/lib/store.ts` (`ServerStore`).
- `authoring/tools/save_file.py` (new), `authoring/server/overview.py`, `authoring/tools/qgen.py` (report), `authoring/server/main.py` (`apply_flags`).
- Docs: `plans/decisions.md`, `plans/12-client-engine.md`, `directory.md`; `.gitignore` already covers `state/`.

## Safety (real save)
- Never run `migrate()` with `TRIVIA_STATE` pointing at the real `state/` during this plan. Every check sets `TRIVIA_STATE` to a `tempfile.mkdtemp()` before importing `paths`, and asserts `save.FILE` is inside it (like `record_fixtures.py`).
- The real-save rehearsal copies `state/game.sqlite` into the temp dir first. It records size, mtime and sha256 of the original before and after, and they must be identical.

## Tasks to split into
1. **`save.py` store + `/api/save`**: `load`, `write` (envelope check, tmp + fsync + replace, `PermissionError` retry → 503), the routes in `main.py`, the docstring, the decision entry. Check with a throw-away script against a server on a temp `TRIVIA_STATE`: 404 before any PUT; PUT/GET round trip byte-equal after parse; bad bodies → 400 (not an object, version 2, missing list, > 10 MB); a simulated lock (monkeypatched `os.replace` raising `PermissionError` 3× then OK → 200; always → 503, old file intact); no `.tmp` left behind.
2. **Migration `migrate()`** (PORT-5): as above, not wired into startup. Throw-away checks in a temp dir: (a) a DB built by today''s `game.init` with players, a running game, per-player and global burns, a deleted player''s leftovers; (b) a pre-`player_id` games table; (c) a pre-D-28 `burned` table (becomes global burns); (d) `flags.json` present/absent; (e) `game.json` already present → no-op; (f) neither file → no-op. Every case: the source DB is byte-identical afterwards, and the row counts and `state` objects match a direct SQLite read.
3. **TS save + stores** (needs task 76): `flags` in `SaveData`/`emptySave()`, `engine/save.ts`, `engine/store.ts` (`MemoryStore`: deep copies in and out, so callers can''t mutate the stored doc), `lib/store.ts` (`ServerStore`: 404 → `emptySave()`, other errors throw with the server''s `error` code; serialized/coalesced PUTs). `save.test.ts`: `parseSave` accepts `emptySave()` and a fixture `db` + version, rejects version 2 and broken shapes, fills missing `flags`; `setFlag` keeps the first `flagged_on`, unflag removes it, the input stays unchanged; `MemoryStore` round trip and isolation. `ServerStore` checked once with a throw-away script against task 1''s server (rapid saves end with the newest document). `npm test`, `npm run check` pass.
4. **Authoring readers**: `save_file.py`, `overview.py`, `qgen report`, `apply_flags` + `state/flags-applied.json`. Throw-away check on a temp `TRIVIA_STATE` with a hand-written `game.json`: start-page players and supply per player, `qgen report --player <name>` (casefold «Íngrid»), an unknown player → "unknown player"; `apply_flags` turns a flag into a review once, a second start skips it, a reflag (new `flagged_on`) applies again, `game.json` byte-identical afterwards. The same checks without `game.json` show today''s behaviour unchanged.
5. **Real-save rehearsal + docs** (needs task 3 and plan 20''s `api.ts`, task 79): throw-away script, temp `TRIVIA_STATE`, copy of `state/game.sqlite` (+ `flags.json` if present), `migrate()`. Then compare Python vs TS on the result: `game.players()` vs `getPlayers`; `burned_ids` per player vs the TS burned set; for the running game (if any) `game.view` vs `getGame` with deterministic shuffles (tape from a patched `random.shuffle`, as in plan 18); `parseSave` accepts the file. Original unchanged (checksum). Then `plans/12-client-engine.md` (PORT-4/PORT-5 `[x]` with notes, the "Migration" switch-over hand-off), `directory.md` (`state/game.json`, `state/flags-applied.json`, new files). Report the counts in the task note, not the save content.

## Done when
- `GET/PUT /api/save` work as specified; writes are atomic and survive a simulated Windows lock.
- `migrate()` turns every schema `game.init` knows, plus `flags.json`, into a `game.json` that `parseSave` accepts; the source files are never modified.
- The rehearsal on a copy of the family''s save shows the same players, burns and running-game view in Python and TS; the original''s checksum is unchanged.
- `trivia-authoring` start page, `qgen report` and `apply_flags` work with and without `game.json`, and never write it.
- `npm test`, `npm run check`, `npm run build`, `qgen validate` pass; no new dependencies.', '2026-10-12T03:21:25Z', 58);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (50, 2, 'Screens on the engine (PORT-7)', 500, 'The game screens stop talking to the Python referee and run the TypeScript engine in the browser. `lib/api.ts` keeps its function signatures (`fetchGame`, `gameAction`, `playJoker`, `fetchPlayers`, `deletePlayer`, `fetchFlags`, `setFlag`), but each one now calls `engine/api.ts` on an in-memory save and persists it through `ServerStore` (`/api/save`). The client loads `pool.json` and the categories files as static files. The same change turns on plan 49''s `game.sqlite` → `game.json` migration at server start. Part 5 of masterplan 2 (PORT-7); part 6 (task 60, PORT-8) deletes the Python game routes afterwards. Source: `plans/12-client-engine.md`, PORT-7.

## Depends on
- Plan 19 (PORT-2): `rng.ts` (`seededRng`, `randomSeed`), `pool.ts` (`parsePool`, `loadCategories`).
- Plan 20 (PORT-3): `engine/api.ts` (`getGame`, `getPlayers`, `deletePlayer`, `gameAction` → `{status, body, save}`), `engineData`, `SaveData`.
- Plan 49 (PORT-4/5): `engine/save.ts` (`parseSave`, `setFlag`, `flaggedIds`), `lib/store.ts` (`ServerStore`), `app/server/save.py` (`migrate()`, `/api/save`), `authoring/tools/save_file.py` (authoring reads `game.json` once it exists).
- Plan 27 (age groups, 590), if it has landed first: `engineData` also takes `age-groups.json` / `bundles.json`. The loader then fetches them too; nothing else here changes.
- Plan 29 (choice screen, 400) builds on this plan: it needs the data files in the client and `lib/api.ts` on the engine.
- If a dependency''s files aren''t there yet, the task waits. No Python fallback.

## Decisions taken here
- **Data files: the server serves `app/data/` read-only at `/data/…`**, not copied by the Vite build.
  - Why: `qgen export` → reload the page is enough, with no `npm run build` after every batch. `dist/` can''t hold a stale pool, and `.#app` already ships `app/data/`.
  - `GET /data/<path>` in `app/server/main.py`: files under `paths.DATA` only (resolve + `is_relative_to`, like `send_static`), else `404 not_found`. GET only. It counts as "static files" (pm decision 4, D-59).
  - Vite dev proxy: add `"/data"` next to `"/api"` and `"/media"`.
  - URLs: `/data/pool.json`, `/data/bundles.json`, `/data/categories.json`, `/data/bundles/<id>/categories.json` (only bundles listed in `bundles.json`; a 404 means the bundle has no categories, like `categories.load()`).
- **Load once per page load.** `lib/engine.ts` (new) has `ready(): Promise<{data, save}>`, memoized: data files + `store.load()` in parallel, then `engineData(...)`, `parseSave(...)`. Python re-read `pool.json` per request; now a new export needs a page reload (F5). Note it in AGENTS.md "Pool" and README.
  - Load failure (server down, missing `pool.json`, newer save version): throw a readable `Error`, which the screens show the way they show a failed fetch today.
- **`lib/api.ts` stays the screens'' only door.** Same exports, same types, same errors, so `Game.svelte`, `Player.svelte` and `Question.svelte` don''t change (at most an import path).
  - One action at a time: a promise-chain mutex in `lib/engine.ts` (replaces the server''s `_lock`). The UI already awaits each call, so the mutex only guards double clicks.
  - Per action: `io = {rng: seededRng(randomSeed()), now: localIsoSeconds()}`. `now` must match Python''s `datetime.now().isoformat(timespec="seconds")`: local time, no zone, `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS`. Put it in `lib/engine.ts`, not the engine (no clock in the engine).
  - Status mapping, like `failure()` today: 200 → body; 400/409/404 with `error` → `GameError(code, params, message, supply)`. An exception thrown by the engine → `GameError("server_error", {}, "engine error: …", null)`, like the Python 500 body.
  - 200 → adopt the new save, then `await store.save(save)`. A failed save keeps the new in-memory save (the next action PUTs the whole document again) and throws `GameError(<server code, e.g. save_locked>)`, so the TV shows something is wrong. Non-200 → the save is unchanged and no PUT.
  - Read-only calls (`fetchGame`, `fetchPlayers`, `fetchFlags`) never PUT. `getGame` may return `withDefaults`-filled state; don''t save on read, as in Python.
  - Flags: `fetchFlags` → `flaggedIds(save)`; `setFlag` → `engine/save.ts` `setFlag(save, …, now)`, store, return `flaggedIds`. `/api/flags` is no longer called.
- **`fetchCategories` leaves `app/client/src/lib/api.ts`**: the game no longer needs `/api/categories`. Move it into `authoring/ui/src/lib/api.ts` (the authoring server keeps its own `/api/categories`). `call`, `json`, `post`, `GameError` stay exported for authoring.
- **Media unchanged:** `Question.svelte` keeps `/media?url=…`. Whether it becomes a plain static path (no download on miss, decision 4) is part 6 (task 60).
- **Switch-over in the same change** (plan 49 hand-off): `main.py` calls `save.migrate()` once before `serve_forever`. From then on the game writes only `state/game.json`; `game.sqlite` and `flags.json` stay as backups. The Python game routes stay until part 6 but nothing calls them. Rollback (plan 49): delete `game.json`, check out the commit before the switch.
- **Decision entry** (next D number in `plans/decisions.md`): the game data comes from `/data/` (read-only, served from `app/data/`, loaded once per page). The engine runs in the browser behind `lib/api.ts`. Each action is persisted by a whole-document PUT. The migration runs at server start from this change on.

## Files
- `app/server/main.py`: `/data/` route, `save.migrate()` at startup, docstring (routes, "reads app/data/ and serves it under /data/").
- `app/client/vite.config.ts`: proxy `/data`.
- `app/client/src/lib/engine.ts` (new): `ready()`, mutex, `io()`, `run(route)` (status mapping, persist).
- `app/client/src/lib/data.ts` (new): `loadEngineData()`, fetches the data files and builds `engineData`.
- `app/client/src/lib/api.ts`: game, player and flag functions on the engine; `fetchCategories` removed.
- `authoring/ui/src/lib/api.ts`: own `fetchCategories`.
- Screens (`app/client/src/game/*.svelte`): only if an import has to move; no markup, style or behaviour changes. `PLACEHOLDER(...)` (D-25) stays.
- Docs: `plans/decisions.md`, `plans/12-client-engine.md` (PORT-7), `directory.md` (new `lib/` files, `/data/`), AGENTS.md and README.md (reload after `qgen export`; `trivia` writes `state/game.json`).

## Safety (real save)
- Workers never start `trivia` on the real `state/` in this plan: the first start migrates for real. Every check sets `TRIVIA_STATE` (and `TRIVIA_MEDIA` if media is touched) to a temp dir and asserts it before starting the server.
- Rehearsals use a copy of `state/game.sqlite` (+ `flags.json`), with the original''s checksum compared before and after (as in plan 49).
- The real switch happens when the gamemaster starts `trivia` after pulling; task 3 tells them so in the decision body.

## Tasks to split into
1. **`/data/` route and data loader**: the route, Vite proxy, `lib/data.ts`, `fetchCategories` moved to authoring. Can land before the switch (screens still on Python). Check with a throw-away script against a server on a temp `TRIVIA_STATE`: `/data/pool.json` and every categories file 200, `/data/../server/main.py`, `/data/%2e%2e/…` and a missing file 404; `loadEngineData()` (run under `node` with a `fetch` shim that prefixes the server URL) gives the same category order as `categories.load()`. Then `trivia-authoring` start page and `/stats/categories` still load. `npm run check`, `npm run build`.
