@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+# Translation runs
+
+The question above is a translation of an approved Spanish question. You see the Spanish one as `source`, and the translator''s `notes`. The source was reviewed already: judge the translation, as the players will meet it. Everything above applies to the translation, with these changes.
+
+## Changes
+- **Voice:** "Language" means the section above for this locale, not Spanish. Report wording that isn''t natural for its players (a literal idiom, translationese, the wrong spelling or unit convention) as `style`.
+- **Media:** the media is the source''s picked file; there are no candidates and no new search. Leave `pick`, `background_pick` and `new_query` null. Judge the file only for its text (below).
+- **Language of your answer:** `feedback`, `fix` and `notes` in English.
+- **The source stays as it is.** A `fix` changes only the translation. If the source itself is wrong, report `fact` and say "source:" in your feedback or notes; leave `fix` empty.
+
+## Check on top
+- **Fidelity:** the translation asks the same fact with the same answer as the source. A changed fact, number or answer is `fact`; a lost detail that made the clue unique, or an added one that gives it away, is `ambiguity` or `giveaway`.
+- **Options:** a wrong answer that became correct or a synonym of the answer is `ambiguity`; one that stands out by grammar (an article, a gender, a case ending) or is absurd enough to rule itself out is `giveaway`.
+- **Hints:** a hint that still plays on the Spanish word, or names the answer in the target language before hint 3, is `giveaway`.
+- **Description:** a literal joke that makes no sense is `style`; one that gives the answer away is `giveaway`.
+- **Media text:** give `media_text`: true when the file likely shows Spanish text the players would read, else false. Not a problem on its own (it is only flagged), except when the text names the answer, or essential media can''t be played without reading it: then `media`.
+
+## Not travelling
+An item without a translation comes with the translator''s `reason` (`wordplay`, `about_spanish`, `needs_local_knowledge`, `other`). Decide in one line:
+- `approved`: right, it doesn''t travel; it stays in Spanish only.
+- `needs_work`: it can be translated; `feedback` says how in one line. It is translated in the next run.
diff --git a/authoring/tools/prompts/translate.md b/authoring/tools/prompts/translate.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..381cb7f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/authoring/tools/prompts/translate.md
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+# Task: translate questions
+
+You translate approved Spanish questions of the trivia game described above into the language of the "Language" section, for its players. The Spanish questions were written, rated, fact-checked and reviewed already. Your translation is rated, fact-checked and reviewed again. Adapt, don''t translate literally: the players must meet the same question as a Spanish family does, in natural words of their own language.
+
+## Input
+A list of questions from one subcategory, each with its `id`, `subcategory`, `difficulty`, the text fields (`description`, `question`, `answer`, `wrong_answers`, `hints`, `fun_fact`) and its `media` (type, role, and the title and description of the file the players see).
+
+## Keep
+- **The same question:** the same fact, the same correct answer, the same clue. Don''t add facts, don''t drop a detail that makes the question unambiguous, don''t make it easier or harder on purpose.
+- **The same answer**, in the form the players know it: "Cristóbal Colón" → "Christopher Columbus"; a film or book by its release title in the market ("Intensa-Mente" → "Inside Out"). A name without a form of its own stays as it is ("Gabriel García Márquez", "arepa").
+- **Don''t fix the source.** If a Spanish fact looks wrong to you, translate it anyway and say so in `notes` ("source fact doubtful: …").
+
+## Adapt
+- **Units, numbers, dates, spelling:** as the "Language" section says.
+- **Idioms:** an idiom becomes the target language''s idiom, or plain words; never a literal copy.
+- **Wrong answers:** each one stays plausible, the same form and similar length as the answer, and clearly wrong. After translating, check the four options again in the target language:
+  - none became correct or a synonym of the answer (two Spanish words can share one translation);
+  - none stands out by grammar: an article ("a"/"an", "der"/"die"/"das"), a gender or case ending that fits only one option;
+  - none became absurd.
+  Replace a wrong answer that fails with another of the same kind, and say so in `notes`.
+- **Hints:** keep the order from vague to strong. A hint that plays on the Spanish word (first letter, rhyme, syllables, gender, a Spanish saying) is rewritten to do the same job in the target language. Hint 3 must not contain the answer in the target language.
+- **Description:** rewrite the joke so it is funny in the target language: the same idea where it works, a new joke about the same content where it doesn''t. It must not give the answer away or rule out options; watch articles and genders here too.
+- **Fun fact:** translated, units adapted.
+- **Length:** house style "Keep it short" holds in the target language; don''t pad.
+- **Media mentioned in the text** ("Escucha:…", "En la foto…") is translated naturally. The file stays as it is.
+
+## Doesn''t travel
+Some questions can''t be asked fairly in the target language. Then answer `travels: "no"`, give the reason and leave the text fields empty:
+- `wordplay`: the question or its clue lives on a Spanish pun or sound that can''t be rebuilt;
+- `about_spanish`: it asks about the Spanish language itself (a spelling, a Spanish plural, what a Spanish word means);
+- `needs_local_knowledge`: the answer only makes sense to someone who lives in the Spanish-speaking world;
+- `other`: say what in `notes`.
+Don''t give up early: a language question with a clean equivalent travels (the word for opposites → "antonym"), and a question that is only harder for these players travels too (the rater re-rates it). Don''t force one that doesn''t: a strained translation costs more than a missing question.
+
+## Media text
+`media_text`: true when the title or description suggests the file shows Spanish text the players would read (a sign, a poster, a book cover, a caption), or essential audio has spoken Spanish; else false. Answer it for every question; it doesn''t change your translation.
+
+## Answer
+For every input `id`, one item:
+- `id`; `travels`: `"yes"` or `"no"`; `reason`: empty for yes, else one of the reasons above.
+- `description`, `question`, `answer`, `wrong_answers` (3), `hints` (3), `fun_fact`: in the target language; empty when `travels` is no.
+- `media_text`: as above.
+- `notes`: in English, one short line per adaptation that a reviewer should know about (a unit changed, a wrong answer replaced, a hint rewritten, a doubtful source fact); empty if you only translated.
```', 'Approve as drafted, except: text in media (Spanish or any language) is never a problem. Drop media_text from translate.md and translate-review.md (no flag, no ''media'' problem for text in media; the ''Media text'' sections go).', NULL, '2026-10-12T19:18:26Z', '2026-10-12T22:11:11Z', 437, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-12, task 438: 2026-10-12, task 438: media_text dropped from translate.md, translate-review.md and TRANSLATE_SCHEMA (no media_text in the locale file or the tr-* report); house-style.md Language block → prompts/locale-es.md, prompt(..., locale=) fills {locale}; qgen translate (TRANSLATE_SCHEMA, chunks of 15 per subcategory), rate/factcheck/merge read run.json locale; a re-translated stale entry keeps its old review as top-level previous.

2026-10-12, task 441: 2026-10-12, task 441: docs follow the answer: D-87 (plans/decisions.md) says text in media is never a problem (no media_text flag, no media problem); 15b''s ''Media'' bullet no longer says the review flags visible text per locale; QC.md''s translation-run checks say the same. RUNBOOK, QC.md, directory.md, AGENTS.md, 15b (QT-2, QT-3 [x]) describe qgen batch --translate.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (36, 'GameRow.locale: written only for non-es games?', 'answered', '[]', 'Keeps the family''s saves and the Python fixture replay byte-identical (plan 68: the family''s game stays as today). The lib layer runs ''new'' on the language''s pool (scope next), every other route on the newest game''s locale; newGame throws if the pool''s locale differs from language.questions.', 'Yes: newGame writes locale only when it isn''t es; missing = es', NULL, '2026-10-12T19:41:19Z', '2026-10-12T19:41:19Z', 449, NULL, 'rule', 449, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (37, 'Per-locale content targets and gap aiming', 'answered', '[]', 'Plan 70 (CT-4). D-75 fixes one mix for every language; the simulation is information only (pm decision 21); per-level mix already covers age-group windows (D-76). Translations can only fill a locale from es sources, so the source shortfall is the one new input to D-54''s weights.', 'Every launch language uses targets.json base''s numbers (D-75), deficits counted per locale; no per-mode, per-age-group or per-preset targets; simulation bottlenecks are information; once es meets its target, the en/de source shortfall drives es drafting', NULL, '2026-10-12T19:46:07Z', '2026-10-12T19:46:07Z', 248, NULL, 'rule', 248, '2026-10-12, task 462: 2026-10-12, task 462: targets.deficits(..., locale), source_supply/source_shortfall/draft_gaps in targets.py; target_weights and batches.subcategories use draft_gaps; qgen report --locale, --simulate target check, --cost (es sources past target) per locale; D-84 in plans/decisions.md; --translate weight is task 464.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (38, 'Base bundles and bundle languages', 'answered', '[]', 'Plan 71, D-85 in plans/decisions.md. Supersedes D-68''s ''every always_on bundle is always on'' and its auto-adding of base: [] and [colombia] are refused (base_bundle_count). A bundle not listed for the game''s locale is refused (bundle_not_offered). qgen validate: errors for missing/invalid languages, leftover always_on, unknown kind, a launch language without a base bundle; warnings for listed bundles without approved questions in pool.<locale>.json and approved questions of unlisted bundles.', 'kind: base replaces always_on; exactly one base bundle per session; bundles offered per languages; default = the locale''s first base bundle plus every non-base bundle offered in the locale; stored choices that no longer resolve read as the locale''s defaults (never written back)', NULL, '2026-10-12T20:39:19Z', '2026-10-12T20:39:19Z', 481, NULL, 'rule', 481, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (39, 'Local builds start offline?', 'pending', '["both pass", "issues (fill the tables)"]', 'Start test of the local desktop builds (SW-5, plan 56), build commit 06c39dc (version 0.1.0).
Artefacts: `trivia-desktop-build` → app/desktop/release/Trivia-0.1.0-win-x64.zip and Trivia-0.1.0-linux-x64.tar.gz (or copy those over).
Checklist and tables to fill: plans/18-steam-integration.md, section "Local build start test".

Windows x64 without Node/Python, and Linux x64 without Nix (e.g. Ubuntu 24.04):
- Network off before unpacking
- Unpack and start: Trivia.exe (SmartScreen «More info → Run anyway» expected) / ./trivia
- No console window (Windows)
- Starts fullscreen; F11 toggles
- Keyboard-only game to level 3 with an image, an audio and a video question; sound plays
- «Salir del juego», restart: player still there
- Save at %APPDATA%\trivia\save\game.json / ~/.config/trivia/save/game.json
- CREDITS.md in the folder (n/a until plan 47)

On issues: name the failing rows and machine (or fill the tables in the plan file).', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-12T21:18:46Z', NULL, 331, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (40, 'English name for the hint joker «Soplo»?', 'answered', '["Psst", "Little Bird", "Nudge", "Tip-off", "Whisper"]', 'Spanish «Soplo»: the classroom whisper (soplar = whisper the answer to a classmate). Joker: reveals the next of the question''s three hints, vague → strong; hint notes are labelled «Soplo 1/2/3». Light-bulb icon. Candidates keep the sneaky-whisper idea. Not a literal translation (15a: joker names are content). ≤ 13 characters (tray width, «Francotirador»). The name also shows in «Use {name}?», the aria label, «no … left» errors and the printable cards. Key letter stays as in es (S, P, F, T, X) until LUI-4. Answer with one option or your own name.
- Psst: the classroom whisper itself, cheeky, 4 chars; notes read «Psst 1/2/3»
- Little Bird: «a little bird told me», warm, family tone
- Nudge: a gentle push toward the answer
- Tip-off: a sly insider tip, a bit spy-like
- Whisper: plain and clear, least playful', 'Hint', NULL, '2026-10-12T21:55:04Z', '2026-10-12T22:29:13Z', 102, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (41, 'English name for the skip joker «Paso»?', 'answered', '["Hard Pass", "Nope", "Next!", "Dodge", "Bye-Bye"]', 'Spanish «Paso»: «I pass». Joker: skips the current question (a new one from the pool), optionally purging its whole subcategory from later draws («Paso, y fuera el tema …»). Door/arrow icon. Not a literal translation (15a: joker names are content). ≤ 13 characters (tray width, «Francotirador»). The name also shows in «Use {name}?», the aria label, «no … left» errors and the printable cards. Key letter stays as in es (S, P, F, T, X) until LUI-4. Answer with one option or your own name.
- Hard Pass: the cheeky idiom for a firm no; keeps P as a matching key
- Nope: shortest, most cheeky; «Nope, and out goes the topic …» reads well
- Next!: the barber''s call, brisk
- Dodge: ducking the question, playground feel
- Bye-Bye: waving the question off, kid-friendly', 'Pass & Purge', NULL, '2026-10-12T21:55:04Z', '2026-10-12T22:29:13Z', 102, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (42, 'English name for the easier joker «Bájale»?', 'answered', '["Dial Down", "Ease Up", "Go Easy", "Simmer Down", "Baby Steps"]', 'Spanish «Bájale»: «turn it down / take it down a notch», colloquial imperative. Joker: swaps the current question for an easier one from the same subcategory; its animation is a difficulty dial («Dificultad») turning down. Down-stairs icon. Not a literal translation (15a: joker names are content). ≤ 13 characters (tray width, «Francotirador»). The name also shows in «Use {name}?», the aria label, «no … left» errors and the printable cards. Key letter stays as in es (S, P, F, T, X) until LUI-4. Answer with one option or your own name.
- Dial Down: matches the dial animation, casual imperative like bájale
- Ease Up: casual plea, same register
- Go Easy: «go easy on us», warm family tone
- Simmer Down: cheeky, playful scolding
- Baby Steps: playful, but may sound condescending to adults', 'Fairy', NULL, '2026-10-12T21:55:04Z', '2026-10-12T22:29:13Z', 102, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (43, 'English name for the category joker «Cambiazo»?', 'answered', '["Switcheroo", "Topic Hop", "Plot Twist", "Swap Shop"]', 'Spanish «Cambiazo»: a sneaky swap (dar el cambiazo = swap something behind someone''s back). Joker: swaps the current question for one of similar difficulty from a subcategory the players choose (dialog «Cambiazo: ¿qué tema quieren?»). Compass/wheel icon. Not a literal translation (15a: joker names are content). ≤ 13 characters (tray width, «Francotirador»). The name also shows in «Use {name}?», the aria label, «no … left» errors and the printable cards. Key letter stays as in es (S, P, F, T, X) until LUI-4. Answer with one option or your own name.
- Switcheroo: exactly the sneaky-swap tone of cambiazo, fun to say
- Topic Hop: says what it does, light
- Plot Twist: dramatic, playful
- Swap Shop: rhyming, cheerful', 'Ms Picky', NULL, '2026-10-12T21:55:04Z', '2026-10-12T22:29:14Z', 102, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (44, 'English name for the snipe joker «Francotirador»?', 'answered', '["Trick Shot", "Sharpshooter", "Long Shot", "Pot Shot"]', 'Spanish «Francotirador»: sniper. Joker: the players aim at the answer they think is false; a wrong answer hit is struck out and play goes on; hitting the right answer loses the question and the level repeats with fresh cards. Crosshair icon. Not a literal translation (15a: joker names are content). ≤ 13 characters (tray width, «Francotirador»). The name also shows in «Use {name}?», the aria label, «no … left» errors and the printable cards. Key letter stays as in es (S, P, F, T, X) until LUI-4. Answer with one option or your own name.
- Trick Shot: playful, the trick is shooting a wrong answer
- Sharpshooter: clear for kids, 12 chars, closest to the Spanish
- Long Shot: pun on a risky bet, fits the repeat-the-level risk
- Pot Shot: a casual, opportunistic shot, cheeky', 'Trick Shot', NULL, '2026-10-12T21:55:04Z', '2026-10-12T22:29:14Z', 102, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (45, 'Health thresholds and selection order for qgen batch --translate runs', 'answered', '[]', 'Plan 66 left the subset to the worker. Drafting metrics (drops, slots, axes, media share, fun) don''t exist for translations; $ per approved''s bound (0.35) is sized for drafting. One-at-a-time balancing gives ~2-3 sources per subcategory at TRANSLATE_SIZE 300; the offset keeps a 50-source pilot spread over levels 1-15 instead of all easiest.', 'tr-* runs: needs_work rate and needs_work per problem as for batches; rejected rate (confirmed not travelling / sources) under drop rate''s bound; audit/review agreement >= quality.min_agreement once >= spot_check_size are audited; $ per approved shown, not a threshold. The pilot tr-<locale>-1 always stops the next run until qgen health ack. Selection: stale first, then one source at a time from the subcategory with the fewest approved translations (picks count), van der Corput order over difficulty with a per-subcategory offset.', NULL, '2026-10-12T22:29:46Z', '2026-10-12T22:29:46Z', 440, NULL, 'rule', 440, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (46, 'Gamemaster mode: level ranges for custom difficulty and for a number of levels other than 12', 'answered', '[]', 'Decision 33 says levels ramp evenly but not how; plan 13 ''Configure game screen'' (task 390). Keeps age-groups.json at 12 levels (D-38), no new data.', 'Custom: level i of n targets start + (end-start)(i-1)/(n-1), range [floor, ceil]. Age group with n levels: level i uses the group''s step round(1 + 11(i-1)/(n-1)); n=1 step 1, n>12 repeats steps.', NULL, '2026-10-12T22:36:39Z', '2026-10-12T22:36:39Z', 390, NULL, 'rule', 390, '');

